{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Root cause analysis — CONFIRMED (Phase 1 exit)\n\n### Proven root cause\n\n**rebar's MCP server emits nanosecond timestamps as bare JSON numbers outside the JSON-interoperable\ninteger range, on an interface whose supported clients all parse JSON numbers as IEEE-754 binary64.**\n\nThe changeable artifacts:\n\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py:31-36` — `class _Out(BaseModel)` sets `model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"allow\")`.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py:101-126` — `TicketStateOut` inherits it and declares **no** timestamp fields, so\n  `created_at` / `updated_at` ride through undeclared, straight from the store, as Python `int`.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py:292-294` (`list_tickets`) and `:231` (`show_ticket`) return those models.\n- Nested raw-dict fields carry more: `comments: list[dict]` (`_mcp_models.py:115`), plus `attestations.*.signed_at`\n  and `signature.signed_at`, all unvalidated pass-through.\n- The values are `time.time_ns()` — 19 digits, e.g. this ticket's true `created_at` is `1787856371950409998`.\n\nBoth halves of the MCP response carry them, via two independent serializers that operate on the same Python\nobject (`mcp/server/fastmcp/utilities/func_metadata.py:120` → `_convert_to_content` → `pydantic_core.to_json`\nat `:538` for the `content` text block; `:129-130` `model_validate` + `model_dump(mode=\"json\")` for\n`structuredContent`). Measured on the real stdio bytes: 14 unsafe integers in the failing `list_tickets` call,\npresent in **both** blocks.\n\n### The client half (measured, Node v22.23.2)\n\n```\nJSON.parse('{\"created_at\":1786649271159032001}').created_at\n  -> 1786649271159032000                                  // Number — silently truncated\nJSON.stringify({x: BigInt(\"1786649271159032001\")})\n  -> TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt      // exact reported string\n```\n\nGitHub Copilot CLI 1.0.80 (`/opt/homebrew/Caskroom/copilot-cli/1.0.80/copilot`, Mach-O, 139.5MB) ships a\nwasm-bindgen bridge with `__wbindgen_bigint`, `BigInt.asUintN`, `JSON.rawJSON`/`isRawJSON` support, and the\nguard `if ('number' == typeof(field) && !Number.isSafeInteger(field)) return true;`. That is a lossless\nnumber path that yields `BigInt` for out-of-safe-range integers; re-stringifying the tool result then throws.\n(Binary-strings evidence: consistent with the mechanism, not by itself proof of the MCP parse path — the\ndecisive evidence is the wire bytes plus the pass/fail split below.)\n\n### Discriminating experiments\n\n**1. Prediction test — which tools must fail.** If the cause is a field common to *ticket records*, the split is\npredictable. Measured from real JSON-RPC stdio bytes:\n\n| tool | unsafe ints | predicted | observed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `ready_tickets` | 144 | FAIL | **FAILS** (operator) |\n| `list_tickets(open,epic)` | 14 | FAIL | **FAILS** (report) |\n| `show_ticket` | 2 | FAIL | not yet exercised |\n| `search` | 0 | pass | — |\n| `summary` | 0 | pass | — |\n| `ticket_deps` | 0 | pass | — |\n| `explain_criterion` | 0 | pass | — |\n| `grounding_info` | 0 | pass | — |\n\nThe split is explained by the models: `SearchResultOut` (`_mcp_models.py:138-141`) sets `extra=\"forbid\"` and\ndeclares no timestamps; the failing tools all return `extra=\"allow\"` ticket records. No observation contradicts it.\n\n**2. Rival: response-size limit / timeout — REFUTED.** The failing `list_tickets(open,epic)` JSON-RPC line is\n**50,532 bytes**. Claude Code *succeeded* on `list_tickets(status=in_progress)` at ~219,000 bytes — a payload\n**~4.3× larger**. Size does not discriminate. The error is a serialization *type* error, not a size or timeout error.\n\n**3. Rival: the `epic` filter / `list_tickets` filtering path — REFUTED** by `ready_tickets`, which takes no\nfilter and fails identically.\n\n**4. Rival: a genuine server-side error — REFUTED.** Driving the same call against the real server over stdio\nreturns `isError: false` with a well-formed result in 3.7s. The server never raises.\n\n**5. Live corroboration of the silent-corruption half, from this session.** The true stored value is\n`created_at = 1787856371950409998`. The MCP wire emits that integer exactly. What the Claude Code client\n*displayed* was `1787856371950410000` — a value ending in zeros, the signature of a float64 round-trip. So the\n\"working\" client is not working; it is losing the low digits without any error.\n\n### Legitimacy: `defect` — and it is BOTH sides, not one\n\nAn adversarial pass was run specifically to build the strongest case that rebar is *not* at fault. That case has\nreal components — MCP's transport spec imposes only UTF-8; JSON-RPC 2.0's only numeric SHOULD concerns fractional\nparts; a client that opts into a lossless parser owns its own round-trip; `docs/bug-creation-contract.md:44-45`\nalready tells consumers timestamps are nanoseconds. **It does not hold up**, for three cited reasons:\n\n1. **RFC 8259 §6** (verbatim): *\"numbers that are integers and are in the range [-(2**53)+1, (2**53)-1] are\n   interoperable in the sense that implementations will agree exactly on their numeric values.\"* A 19-digit\n   integer is, by the spec's own definition, outside interoperable territory. Emitting one is an affirmative\n   choice to leave it.\n2. **rebar already reached this exact conclusion and acted on it — elsewhere.**\n   `src/rebar/_store/canonical.py:15-19`: *\"jq parses the >2^53 ns `timestamp` as float64 and rounds it … so jq\n   is kept out of the event path entirely.\"* Also `src/rebar/_store/hlc.py:24-25` (*\"**jq must never touch it**\"*),\n   `docs/remediation-implementation-plan.md:74`, and a pinned regression test\n   `tests/unit/test_canonical.py:64-72`. rebar judged float64 rounding of these values severe enough to ban a\n   tool from a code path — then shipped a new surface whose entire supported client population is float64\n   parsers, without generalising the rule.\n3. **The surface is mandated and the client is named.** `AGENTS.md:119-121` requires tracker ops to be routed\n   through MCP; `docs/mcp-client-setup.md:3` and `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md:17-18` name GitHub Copilot CLI as a\n   supported client expected to drive *all* rebar operations. There is no sanctioned fallback when it throws.\n\nCopilot CLI also has a genuine contributing bug (lossless parse followed by a naive `JSON.stringify`). But it is\nnot the root cause, and it does not explain the silent truncation in the other supported client. **Both are\nbroken; only one of them tells you.** Recording that plainly rather than blaming one side.\n\n### CORRECTION to my previous comment on this ticket\n\nMy earlier comment implied the repo had no prior awareness of this hazard. **That was wrong**, and I am correcting\nit rather than leaving it to mislead the next reader. My grep searched for `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` / `BigInt` /\n`\"safe integer\"` and found nothing; rebar expresses the same idea as `>2^53` and `float64`, and *does* document\nand test it (citations 2 above). Only the JavaScript *spelling* was absent, not the knowledge. The finding is\nstrengthened by this, not weakened: it makes the MCP surface an **incomplete generalisation of a hazard rebar had\nalready identified**, not an unknown unknown.\n\n### Defect attribution: UNATTRIBUTABLE (searches recorded)\n\n- `git log --diff-filter=A --follow -- src/rebar/mcp_server.py` → the FastMCP server was introduced in the\n  project's **founding import commit `b534f47a7f9bf216498bac941a28e9b91c3c3189`** (\"Extract DSO ticket system +\n  Jira reconciler into standalone rebar\", 2026-06-08).\n- `git show b534f47a:src/rebar/mcp_server.py` shows `def list_tickets(...) -> list[dict]` returning raw store\n  dicts — so `created_at` went out as a bare 19-digit JSON number **from the first commit**. The later\n  `_mcp_models.py` split (`e7d4710e23b7`) is a **carrier**, not the origin: `extra=\"allow\"` preserved existing\n  behaviour rather than introducing it.\n- `git log -S'time.time_ns' --reverse -- src/rebar/` → same founding commit.\n- `rebar verify-commit-ticket --rev b534f47a7f9b` → *\"commit has no resolvable rebar ticket reference\"* (it\n  predates the trailer convention; the two commits before it are literally `first commit`).\n- Tracker search for an epic covering the MCP server's introduction → **0 results**.\n\nThis is an **omission** (a guard never written) whose origin commit carries no ticket and for which no\nscope-of-work ticket exists. Attribution is therefore left **unset** rather than guessed; a fabricated\n`caused_by` would corrupt the escape-rate metric it feeds. `--caused-by` will not be passed on the close, and\nthis paragraph is the record of why.\n\n### Remediation constraint checked (and one correction to it)\n\n`docs/api-stability.md:23` allows *\"new **optional** keys may be added; required keys are not removed or\nretyped.\"* `created_at` / `updated_at` are **not** in `ticket_state.schema.json`'s `required` list\n(`[\"ticket_id\",\"ticket_type\",\"title\",\"status\",\"priority\",\"tags\"]`), and outputs are `additionalProperties: true`.\nSo the no-retype rule does **not** bind these fields. Separately, `TicketStateOut` does not declare them at all,\nso the advertised MCP `outputSchema` states no type for them — there is no advertised type to contradict.\n\nFix and RED→GREEN evidence follow."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787857474063479140,"uuid":"fbe788a3-1963-4562-b93c-abd9a3553e3a"}